Q: Accessing a function, defined in the encapsulating structure, in the nested template function A: <p>As mentionned in @Jarod42's answer, you need to provide an instance of <code>LoggingClass</code> to the <code>FAEWE_LOG_THREAD</code> constructor if you need to call some non static member function on it.</p>
<p>In order to avoid to change the call site of each <code>FAEWE_LOG_THREAD</code> call with the extra <code>*this</code> argument, you can create an indirection directly in <code>LoggingClass</code> such as (using <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/member_functions.html#Explicit_object_member_functions" rel="nofollow noreferrer">deducing this</a> feature from C++23):</p>
<pre><code>auto  FAEWE_LOG_THREAD (this auto&amp;&amp; self, auto&amp;&amp;...args) {
    FAEWE_LOG_THREAD_impl (self, std::forward&lt;decltype(args)&gt;(args)...);
}
</code></pre>
<p>But by doing so, we won't get the expected function name from the call site. We can rely on a mechanism such as described in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79875667/2229039">this post</a> for broadcasting the actual source location to its final destination; we thus define first</p>
<pre><code>struct FormatWithLocation {
  const char* value;
  std::source_location loc;
  FormatWithLocation(const char* s,
      const std::source_location&amp; l = std::source_location::current())
    : value(s), loc(l) {}
};
</code></pre>
<p>which can be used as follows:</p>
<pre><code>auto  FAEWE_LOG_THREAD (bool fallback,FormatWithLocation fmt, auto&amp;&amp;...args) {
    FAEWE_LOG_THREAD_impl(
       (*this), fmt.loc, fallback, fmt.value,
        std::forward&lt;decltype(args)&gt;(args)...
    );
}

auto  FAEWE_LOG_THREAD (FormatWithLocation fmt, auto&amp;&amp;...args) {
    FAEWE_LOG_THREAD_impl(
       (*this), fmt.loc, fmt.value,
        std::forward&lt;decltype(args)&gt;(args)...
    );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that we have to distingish the two cases, one with the fallback boolean as first parameter and the second one without it.</p>
<p>The final solution leads to <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/rWd7cc9GY" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this proposal</a>.</p>

Q: Why does Combine.PassthroughSubject drop values sent synchronously in handleEvents(receiveSubscription:)? A: <p>Fundamentally, the problem is that, as the <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/combine/passthroughsubject" rel="nofollow noreferrer">docs</a> tell us (emphasis added):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A <code>PassthroughSubject</code> drops values if there are no subscribers, or <em>its current demand is zero.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>So sending values inside the <code>receiveSubscription</code> closure is just too early in the process. The demand is not established until the <code>sink</code>. I’m not sure why you’re using <code>handleEvents</code> at all. Usually one creates the publisher (the <code>PassThroughSubject</code> in this case), add the <code>sink</code>, and only then send values/completion.</p>
<p>I am unaware of any formal assurances that <code>sink</code>  would somehow defer <code>receiveSubscription</code> until after it had also demanded any values. And your example perfectly illustrates that, in practice, this is not the case. It apparently creates a subscriber and attaches it to the publisher (resulting in <code>receiveSubscription</code> being called) and only then demands values. I hear you, that this might not have been what you expected, but this is how it works.</p>
<hr />
<p>In practice, we would generally do:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>import XCTest

class CombineSubscriptionTest: XCTestCase {
    func testSendingInReceiveSubscription() {
        let value = 1

        let subject = PassthroughSubject&lt;Int, Never&gt;()

        let expectation = expectation(description: &quot;test&quot;)
        var collectedValues = [Int]()

        let cancellable = subject.sink { completion in
            expectation.fulfill()
        } receiveValue: { value in
            collectedValues.append(value)
        }

        subject.send(value)
        subject.send(completion: .finished)

        wait(for: [expectation], timeout: 1.0)

        XCTAssertEqual(collectedValues, [value])
    }
}
</code></pre>
